Java 6 boot class path for the BD-J build
=========================================

What this is
------------
rt.jar from **OpenJDK 6 b38** as packaged by Debian (IcedTea 1.13.10), taken
from the binary package:

    openjdk-6-jre-headless_6b38-1.13.10-1~deb7u1_amd64.deb
    sha256 fdfa1442b906e62a46a82a582e48b0b6b3e67611b569606678107536a2e333ce
    http://archive.debian.org/debian/pool/main/o/openjdk-6/

The checksum above is the one published in Debian's own (HTTPS-served) package
indexes for both wheezy/main and wheezy/updates, and was verified on download.
rt.jar is architecture-independent: the amd64 package is only where it happens
to ship. COPYRIGHT is that package's Debian copyright file.

Why it is archived here
-----------------------
The BD-J jars built by contrib/src/bluray must run on the oldest JRE our targets
can offer, which is Java 6 (including the community Java 6 PowerPC port on
10.4+). Two build steps need a real Java 6 class library, and neither should
depend on a network fetch or on an old machine being powered on:

  - javac compiles the BD-J sources with -bootclasspath pointing here, so the
    compiler physically cannot link against an API newer than Java 6;
  - check-bdj-java6.py resolves against this jar the references that the JDK's
    ct.sym cannot describe, because ct.sym only records the *documented* API and
    BD-J's AWT replacement legitimately reaches into package-private and sun.awt
    internals (sun.awt.CausedFocusEvent, sun.awt.ComponentFactory,
    java.awt.EventDispatchThread, java.io.FileSystem and friends).

Both are wired up in contrib/src/bluray/rules.mak through BLURAY_JAVA6_DIR;
every .jar found in this directory joins the boot class path automatically.

This jar is **compile-time only**. Nothing here is copied into the application
bundle, and PowerVLC never redistributes a JRE: BD-J menus require the user to
have installed one.

Licensing
---------
OpenJDK 6 is GPLv2 with the Classpath Exception — redistributable, unlike the
Apple or Oracle runtimes. See COPYRIGHT. GPLv2 section 3 ties redistribution of
the binary to making the corresponding source available; the matching Debian
source package is:

    openjdk-6_6b38-1.13.10.orig.tar.gz
    http://archive.debian.org/debian/pool/main/o/openjdk-6/

Why not Apple's Java 6
----------------------
Apple's classes.jar/ui.jar (1.6.0_65) is what the PowerPC port descends from, so
it is the most faithful reference, but it is proprietary and cannot be shipped
in a public tree. It was used once, off a 10.6.8 machine, to cross-check this
setup: the BD-J jars compiled against the OpenJDK 6 rt.jar below verify clean
against Apple's class library too, every internal reference included. That check
can be repeated at any time by pointing BLURAY_JAVA6_DIR at a local copy of
Apple's Classes/ directory.
